" (https://www.csie.ntu.edu.tw/~kmchao/tree10spr/diameter.pdf)\nciteturn9878view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 4; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.csie.ntu.edu.tw/~kmchao/tree10spr/diameter.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 201\nL0@P0: A note on Eccentricities, diameters, and radii∗\nL1@P0: Bang Ye Wu Kun–Mao Chao\nL2@P0: Let G = (V, E, w) be a graph and U ⊂ V . By DG(v, U), we denote the maximum distance\nL3@P0: from vertex v to any vertex in U. For a vertex v, the eccentricity of v is the maximum of the\nL4@P0: distance to any vertex in the graph, i.e., maxu∈V {dG(v, u)} or DG(v, V ). The diameter of a graph\nL5@P0: is the maximum of the eccentricity of any vertex in the graph. (The term “diameter” is overloaded.\nL6@P0: It is defined as the maximum eccentricity and also as the path of length equal to the maximum\nL7@P0: eccentricity.) In other words, the diameter is the longest distance between any two vertices in the\nL8@P0: graph. Recall that the distance between two vertices is the length of their shortest path in the\nL9@P0: graph. It should not be confused with the longest path in the graph.\nL10@P0: The radius of a graph is the minimum eccentricity among all vertices in the graph, and a center\nL11@P0: of a graph is a vertex with eccentricity equal to the radius. For a general graph, there may be several\nL12@P0: centers and a center is not necessarily on a diameter. For example, in Figure 1(a), the shortest\nL13@P0: path between v1 and v4 is a diameter of length 6. Meanwhile v2 and v3 are endpoints of another\nL14@P0: diameter. The four vertices represented by white circles are centers of the graph, and the radius is\nL15@P0: 4. Note that the centers are not on any diameter. The diameter, radius and center of a graph can\nL16@P0: be found by computing the distances between all pairs of vertices. It takes O(|V ||E| + |V |\nL17@P0: 2\nL18@P0: log |V |)\nL19@P0: time for a general graph.\nL20@P0: 2\nL21@P0: \nL22@P0: 2\nL23@P0: \nL24@P0: x\nL25@P0: \nL26@P0: 7\nL27@P0: \nL28@P0: 3\nL29@P0: \nL30@P0: 1\nL31@P0: \nL32@P0: 6\nL33@P0:  4\nL34@P0: \nL35@P0: 4\nL36@P0: \nL37@P0: 3\nL38@P0: \nL39@P0: 2\nL40@P0: \nL41@P0: 5\nL42@P0:  y\nL43@P0: \nL44@P0: v\nL45@P0: 1\nL46@P0:  v\nL47@P0: 2\nL48@P0: \nL49@P0: v\nL50@P0: 3\nL51@P0:  v\nL52@P0: 4\nL53@P0: \nL54@P0: (a)\nL55@P0:  (b)\nL56@P0: \nL57@P0: Figure 1: Diameters, centers and radii of (a) an unweighted graph; and (b) a weighted tree.\nL58@P0: Any pair of vertices has a unique simple path in a tree. For this reason, the diameter, radius\nL59@P0: and centers of a tree are more related. For an unweighted tree T = (V, E), it can be easily verified\nL60@P0: that\nL61@P0: 2 × radius − 1 ≤ diameter ≤ 2 × radius. (1)\nL62@P0: For positive weighted tree T = (V, E, w), we can also have\nL63@P0: 2 × radius − max\nL64@P0: e\nL65@P0: {w(e)} ≤ diameter ≤ 2 × radius. (2)\nL66@P0: ∗An excerpt from the book “Spanning Trees and Optimization Problems,” by Bang Ye Wu and Kun-Mao Chao\nL67@P0: (2004), Chapman & Hall/CRC Press, USA.\nL68@P0-1: 1Figure 1(b) illustrates an example of the diameter, radius, and center of a tree. The two vertices\nL69@P1: represented by white circles are the centers of the tree. There are four diameters of length 16 in\nL70@P1: the tree. Vertices x and y are the endpoints of a diameter. The radius is 9, and the centers are on\nL71@P1: the diameters.\nL72@P1: More efficient algorithms for computing diameter, radius, and centers are available if the graph\nL73@P1: is a tree. Let T = (V, E, w) be a rooted tree. By Tr, we denote the subtree rooted at vertex r ∈ V ,\nL74@P1: which is the subgraph induced on vertex r and all its descendants. Let child(r) denote the set of\nL75@P1: children of v. The eccentricity of the root of a tree can be computed by the following recurrence\nL76@P1: relation.\nL77@P1: DTr(r, V (Tr)) = max\nL78@P1: s∈child(r)\nL79@P1: {DTs(s, V (Ts)) + w(r, s)}. (3)\nL80@P1: By a recursive algorithm or an algorithm visiting the vertices in postorder, the eccentricity can be\nL81@P1: computed in linear time since each vertex is visited once. To make it clear, we give a recursive\nL82@P1: algorithm in the following.\nL83@P1: Algorithm: Eccent(Tr)\nL84@P1: Input: A tree Tr = (V, E, w) rooted at r.\nL85@P1: Output: The eccentricity of r in Tr.\nL86@P1: 1: if r is a leaf then\nL87@P1: return 0;\nL88@P1: 2: for each child s of r do\nL89@P1: compute Eccent(Ts) recursively;\nL90@P1: 3: return maxs∈child(r){Eccent(s) + w(r, s)}.\nL91@P1: To find the eccentricity of a vertex in an unrooted tree, we can root the tree at the vertex and\nL92@P1: employ the Eccent algorithm. Therefore, we have the next lemma.\nL93@P1: Lemma 1: The eccentricity of a vertex in a tree can be computed in linear time.\nL94@P1: Let x, y and z be three vertices in a tree T. It can be easily verified that the three paths\nL95@P1: SPT (x, y), SPT (x, z) and SPT (y, z) intersect at a vertex. Define c(x, y, z) to be the intersection\nL96@P1: vertex. We have\nL97@P1: dT (x, c(x, y, z)) = 1\nL98@P1: 2\nL99@P1: (dT (x, y) + dT (x, z) − dT (y, z)), (4)\nL100@P1: or equivalently\nL101@P1: dT (x, c(x, y, z)) = 1\nL102@P1: 2\nL103@P1: (dT (x, y) + dT (x, z) + dT (y, z)) − dT (y, z). (5)\nL104@P1: We now derive some properties to help us find the diameter of a tree.\nL105@P1: Fact 1: Suppose that SPT (v1, v2) is a diameter of T and r is a vertex on the diameter. For any\nL106@P1: vertex x, dT (x, r) ≤ max{dT (r, v1), dT (r, v2)}.\nL107@P1: Proof: Otherwise SPT (x, v1) or SPT (x, v2) is a path longer than the diameter.\nL108@P1: The property can be easily extended to the case where r is not on the diameter. Let u =\nL109@P1: c(r, v1, v2). Without loss of generality, let dT (u, v1) ≥ dT (u, v2). For any vertex x, we have\nL110@P1: dT (x, u) ≤ dT (v1, u) by Fact 1. Then\nL111@P1: dT (x, r) ≤ dT (x, u) + dT (u, r) ≤ dT (v1, u) + dT (u, r) = dT (v1, r),\nL112@P1-2: 2which implies that v1 is the farthest vertex to r. We can conclude that, for any vertex, one of the\nL113@P2: endpoints of a diameter must be the farthest vertex. Furthermore the converse of the property is\nL114@P2: also true.\nL115@P2: Let r be any vertex in a tree and v3 be the vertex farthest to r. We shall show that v3 must be\nL116@P2: an endpoint of a diameter. Suppose that SPT (v1, v2) is a diameter. By the above property, one of\nL117@P2: the endpoints, say v1, is the farthest vertex to r, i.e.,\nL118@P2: dT (r, v1) = dT (r, v3) ≥ dT (r, v2).\nL119@P2: Let u1 = c(r, v1, v2) and u2 = c(r, v1, v3). Then u2 must be on the path SPT (v1, u1), for otherwise\nL120@P2: dT (v1, v3) = dT (v1, u2) + dT (u2, v3)\nL121@P2: = 2dT (v1, u2) > 2dT (v1, u1)\nL122@P2: > dT (v1, v2),\nL123@P2: a contradiction. If u1 = u2, then dT (v3, v1) = dT (v1, v2) and SPT (v3, v1) is also a diameter.\nL124@P2: Otherwise, dT (v3, v2) = dT (v1, v2) and SPT (v3, v2) is also a diameter. We have the next lemma.\nL125@P2: Lemma 2: Let r be any vertex in a tree T. If v is the farthest vertex to r, the eccentricity of v\nL126@P2: is the diameter of T.\nL127@P2: The following algorithm uses the property to find the diameter of a tree.\nL128@P2: Algorithm: TreeDiameter\nL129@P2: Input: A tree T = (V, E, w).\nL130@P2: Output: The diameter of T.\nL131@P2: 1: Root T at an arbitrary vertex r.\nL132@P2: 2: Use Eccent to find the farthest vertex v to r.\nL133@P2: 3: Root T at v.\nL134@P2: 4: Use Eccent to find the eccentricity of v.\nL135@P2: 5: Output the eccentricity of v as the diameter of T.\nL136@P2: It is obvious that the algorithm runs in linear time. The radius and the center can be obtained\nL137@P2: from a diameter. Suppose that P = SPT (v1, v2) is a diameter. Starting at v1 and traveling along\nL138@P2: the path P, we compute the distance dT (u, v1) for each vertex u on the path. Let u1 be the last\nL139@P2: encountered vertex such that dT (v1, u1) ≤\nL140@P2: 1\nL141@P2: 2w(P) and u2 be the next vertex to u1 (Figure 2(a)).\nL142@P2: By the definition of u1, u2 is the first encountered vertex such that dT (v1, u2) >\nL143@P2: 1\nL144@P2: 2w(P). We claim\nL145@P2: that u1 or u2 is a center of the tree. Let P1 = SPT (v1, u1) and P2 = SPT (u2, v2). First, by Fact 1,\nL146@P2: the eccentricities of u1 and u2 must be dT (u1, v2) and dT (u2, v1) respectively. Otherwise P cannot\nL147@P2: be a diameter. For any vertex x connected to P at a vertex in P1, we have dT (x, v2) > dT (u1, v2).\nL148@P2: Similarly, for any vertex x connected to P at a vertex in P2, we have dT (x, v1) > dT (u2, v1).\nL149@P2: Consequently the eccentricity of any vertex is at least min{dT (u1, v2), dT (u2, v1)}, and u1 or u2\nL150@P2: must be a center. Therefore the center as well as the radius of a tree can be computed in linear\nL151@P2: time since the diameter can be found in linear time.\nL152@P2: Theorem 3: The diameter, radius, and center of a tree can be computed in linear time.\nL153@P2: Let us examine some more properties of the diameters of a tree. We assume that all edge lengths\nL154@P2: are positive.\nL155@P2-3: 3(a)\nL156@P3:  (b)\nL157@P3: \nL158@P3: v\nL159@P3: 1\nL160@P3:  v\nL161@P3: 2\nL162@P3: \nL163@P3: v\nL164@P3: 3\nL165@P3:  v\nL166@P3: 4\nL167@P3: \nL168@P3: u\nL169@P3: 1\nL170@P3: \nL171@P3: u\nL172@P3: 2\nL173@P3: \nL174@P3: v\nL175@P3: 1\nL176@P3:  u\nL177@P3: 1\nL178@P3:  u\nL179@P3: 2\nL180@P3:  v\nL181@P3: 2\nL182@P3: \nL183@P3: T\nL184@P3: 1\nL185@P3:  T\nL186@P3: 2\nL187@P3: \nL188@P3: Figure 2: (a) Finding a center on a diameter. (b) Two diameters cannot be disjoint.\nL189@P3: Fact 2: Two diameters of a tree cannot be disjoint.\nL190@P3: Proof: Suppose that SPT (v1, v2) and SPT (v3, v4) are two disjoint diameters of a tree T. Let\nL191@P3: u1 = c(v3, v1, v2) and u2 = c(v1, v3, v4) (Figure 2(b)). We have\nL192@P3: dT (v1, v3) + dT (v2, v4) = dT (v1, v2) + dT (v3, v4) + 2dT (u1, u2)\nL193@P3: > 2dT (v1, v2)\nL194@P3: since dT (v1, v2) = dT (v3, v4) is the diameter and dT (u1, u2) > 0. It implies that the path from v1\nL195@P3: to v3 or the path from v2 to v4 is longer than the diameter, a contradiction.\nL196@P3: Let P be a set of more than two paths of a tree and the paths intersect each other. One can\nL197@P3: easily verify that all the paths in P share a common vertex. Otherwise there exists a cycle, which\nL198@P3: contradicts the definition of a tree. Therefore we can have the next property.\nL199@P3: Fact 3: All diameters of a tree share at least one common vertex.\nL200@P3: 4\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn9878view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://hrcak.srce.hr/file/165537\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://hrcak.srce.hr/file/165537: (418) I'm a Teapot\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn9878view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://csikvarip.web.elte.hu/csikvarin2002uj.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://csikvarip.web.elte.hu/csikvarin2002uj.pdf: (400) Timeout fetching\n"
